I'll synthesize these 15 paper analyses into a strategic Field Guide. Let me first organize my thinking by examining cross-cutting patterns, then produce the final JSON.

```json
{
  "innovation_patterns": [
    {
      "pattern": "Unification of retrieval modalities in a single model",
      "description": "Take capabilities that previously required separate models (sparse+dense, embedding+generation, multilingual+monolingual) and show they can coexist in one backbone with no performance degradation. The key is demonstrating zero-sum fears are wrong — modalities are complementary, not competing. Requires careful multi-task loss balancing and often task-specific architectural switches (attention masks, pooling layers, output heads).",
      "examples": ["GritLM (embedding+generation in Mistral-7B)", "BGE M3-Embedding (dense+sparse+multi-vec in XLM-RoBERTa)", "PromptReps (dense+sparse from single LLM forward pass without training)"],
      "success_rate": "high"
    },
    {
      "pattern": "Staged curriculum training replacing monolithic pipelines",
      "description": "Break complex training objectives into 2-3 sequential stages where each stage builds on the previous one's learned representations. Typically: (1) backbone adaptation/pre-training, (2) task-specific contrastive fine-tuning, optionally (3) refinement or distillation. Each stage uses different learning rates, loss functions, and often different parameter subsets (full FT → LoRA). The staging addresses optimization conflicts that arise from jointly optimizing dissimilar objectives from scratch.",
      "examples": ["LACONIC (MNTP+SimCSE → contrastive sparse training)", "LLM2Vec (bidirectional enable → MNTP → SimCSE)", "NV-Embed (retrieval-only stage → blended retrieval+non-retrieval)", "Bidirectional Reconstruction paper (reconstruction → contrastive)"],
      "success_rate": "high"
    },
    {
      "pattern": "Self-distillation eliminating external teacher dependency",
      "description": "Use the model's own representations or predictions as training signal instead of requiring a separate, expensive teacher model (cross-encoder, GPT-4, etc.). The model's pre-trained knowledge serves as an implicit teacher. Works because large pre-trained models encode richer similarity structure than their fine-tuned task performance initially exploits.",
      "examples": ["ICTIR 2025 Adaptive Margins (encoder's own embeddings as margin target)", "BGE M3's self-knowledge distillation between heads", "CIKM 2025 bidirectional self-KD between sparse and dense in VLP"],
      "success_rate": "medium"
    },
    {
      "pattern": "Cross-domain technique transplant to decoder-only IR",
      "description": "Identify a technique proven in a different domain (mechanistic interpretability, vision-language, encoder-only NLP) and adapt it to decoder-only retrieval, solving a known limitation. Requires understanding why the original technique works and what changes when the backbone shifts from encoder-only/other to decoder-only (primarily: causal attention constraints, different pre-training objectives, different token representation semantics).",
      "examples": ["SPLARE (SAEs from interpretability → sparse retrieval)", "Mistral-SPLADE (Echo embeddings to circumvent causal masking)", "CIKM 2025 (bidirectional self-KD from VLP → text retrieval)"],
      "success_rate": "medium"
    },
    {
      "pattern": "Per-query adaptive retrieval routing/weighting",
      "description": "Replace fixed hyperparameters (interpolation weights, retriever selection) with query-conditioned decisions. Can be implemented via LLM scoring (DAT), unsupervised statistical signals (MoR), or learned routing heads (RouterRetriever). The universal finding is that optimal sparse-vs-dense weighting varies dramatically across queries, and even simple adaptive schemes yield +2-7% gains over fixed baselines.",
      "examples": ["DAT (LLM-scored per-query alpha)", "MoR (unsupervised Moran coefficient routing)", "RouterRetriever (learned routing over expert models)"],
      "success_rate": "high"
    },
    {
      "pattern": "Simplification that achieves SOTA by removing pipeline stages",
      "description": "Show that a complex multi-stage pipeline can be replaced by a simpler approach that leverages the LLM's pre-trained knowledge more directly. The pitch is: the field over-engineered this because it started with weaker backbones; stronger backbones make intermediate steps unnecessary. Reviewers reward this when the simplification is principled (not just lazy) and ablations clearly attribute gains to the simplification itself.",
      "examples": ["E5-Mistral (eliminates contrastive pre-training via synthetic data + brief fine-tuning)", "LLM2Vec (3-step unsupervised recipe replaces expensive supervised conversion)", "PromptReps (zero-shot prompting replaces all training)"],
      "success_rate": "high"
    }
  ],
  "reviewer_preferences": [
    "Comprehensive ablation studies isolating each component's contribution: GritLM earned ICLR Oral partly through exhaustive ablations showing embedding and generation don't degrade each other; NV-Embed's Spotlight came despite reviewers noting 'limited novelty of individual components' because the ablation-backed integration story was compelling. Every architectural choice (bidirectional attention, pooling strategy, loss function, curriculum stage) needs its own ablation row.",
    "Zero-shot transfer evaluation on BEIR/MTEB, not just in-domain MS MARCO: E5-Mistral, SPLARE, Mistral-SPLADE, and NV-Embed all lead with BEIR/MTEB results. The ICTIR 2025 self-distillation paper was penalized for poor BEIR transfer (0.341 vs TAS-B's 0.430) despite strong in-domain results. Reviewers treat MS MARCO-only evaluation as a red flag for overfitting.",
    "Compute-awareness and reproducibility: the NeurIPS 2024 scaling laws paper won Best Paper consideration by making compute budgets explicit. E5-Mistral reports '18 hours on 32 V100s'; LLM2Vec reports '~3hrs on a single A100'. Reviewers increasingly expect FLOPs, GPU-hours, and clear hardware specs. Conversely, NV-Embed's 'complex multi-stage pipeline with synthetic data generation' drew reproducibility concerns.",
    "Practical deployment considerations beyond raw accuracy: GritLM's embedding reuse for >60% RAG speedup, RepLLaMA's 135GB index size flagged as impractical, SPLARE's post-hoc Top-K decoupling praised for deployment flexibility. Show inference latency, index size, and memory footprint alongside NDCG.",
    "Clear positioning against strong baselines (not strawmen): DAT only evaluated on 2 small QA datasets with ~3k queries, weakening its claims. The NeurIPS scaling laws paper used only Pythia models, and reviewers questioned transferability. Use the strongest available baselines (BM25+CE reranking, BGE-M3, NV-Embed v2) and evaluate on the full BEIR/MTEB suites.",
    "Novelty framing as 'first to combine X and Y for Z' rather than 'first to invent X': NV-Embed (ICLR Spotlight) and GritLM (ICLR Oral) both succeeded with combination novelty, but required ironclad ablations proving the combination is non-trivial. The bar is: each component alone doesn't achieve the result; the interaction effect is the contribution."
  ],
  "field_gaps": [
    {
      "gap": "No decoder-only LLM produces both sparse AND dense representations from a single forward pass with learned (not prompt-based) heads",
      "evidence": "PromptReps shows it's feasible zero-shot but uses no training; LACONIC and Lion-SP/DS (SIGIR 2025) train separate models for each modality; BGE M3 unifies both but only in encoder-only (XLM-RoBERTa); GritLM unifies embedding+generation but has no sparse head. The TMLR 2025 survey explicitly identifies this as an uncharted point in the architectural design space.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "impact_if_solved": "high"
    },
    {
      "gap": "No learned per-query router exists WITHIN a unified retrieval model — all routing work operates between separate external models",
      "evidence": "RouterRetriever (AAAI 2025) routes between separate dense expert models; MoR (EMNLP 2025) uses unsupervised signals over separate retrievers; DAT (arXiv 2025) requires LLM inference per query. BGE M3 uses fixed interpolation. No work learns a routing function that shares a backbone with the retrieval heads it routes between.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "impact_if_solved": "high"
    },
    {
      "gap": "Scaling laws for sparse retrieval are completely unknown",
      "evidence": "The SIGIR 2024 Best Paper (Scaling Laws for Dense Retrieval) explicitly notes 'sparse retrieval scaling laws are not studied.' All scaling work covers dense retrieval only. No one has characterized how SPLADE-style models scale with model size, data quantity, or compute budget — critical for the proposed 3B vs 8B decision.",
      "difficulty": "high",
      "impact_if_solved": "high"
    },
    {
      "gap": "Bidirectional self-distillation between sparse and dense heads has never been validated in decoder-only architectures",
      "evidence": "BGE M3 uses self-KD in encoder-only models; CIKM 2025 proves it for VLP models in text-image retrieval; ICTIR 2025 shows self-distillation works within a single head. But decoder-only models have fundamentally different attention patterns and pre-training objectives (autoregressive vs. MLM), and CSplade documents that sparse heads in causal LMs face unique instabilities not present in encoder-only models.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "impact_if_solved": "high"
    },
    {
      "gap": "EOS/last-token pooling is used universally for decoder-only dense retrieval but was never designed for this purpose",
      "evidence": "The Bidirectional Reconstruction paper (EMNLP 2025) explicitly identifies that 'the EOS token serves merely as a sequence delimiter during pretraining and was never trained to encode contextual semantics.' NV-Embed's latent attention pooling partially addresses this but adds parameters. No systematic comparison exists of pooling strategies (EOS, mean, latent attention, weighted) specifically for decoder-only retrieval with dual heads.",
      "difficulty": "low",
      "impact_if_solved": "medium"
    },
    {
      "gap": "Compute-optimal training recipes for joint sparse+dense objectives are unknown",
      "evidence": "NeurIPS 2024 scaling laws paper establishes LoRA-vs-full-FT crossover for contrastive-only training but notes 'unclear if findings hold for joint contrastive + sparse (SPLADE) objectives.' The three-stage curriculum introduces additional budget allocation questions (how much compute per stage?) that no existing scaling analysis addresses.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "impact_if_solved": "medium"
    },
    {
      "gap": "SAE-based sparse representations vs. vocabulary-based SPLADE representations have never been compared in a controlled setting within the same backbone",
      "evidence": "SPLARE (arXiv 2026) proposes SAE features as a SPLADE replacement with strong multilingual results, but evaluates on a different backbone and training setup than any SPLADE work. Mistral-SPLADE and LACONIC use vocabulary projection. No apples-to-apples comparison exists, and SPLARE's frozen SAE constraint may conflict with joint training objectives.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "impact_if_solved": "medium"
    }
  ],
  "untried_combinations": [
    {
      "idea_sketch": "Dual sparse+dense heads with a learned router in a single decoder-only LLM, trained via staged curriculum with bidirectional self-distillation — the core proposed work",
      "from_papers": ["LACONIC (sparse head design for decoder-only)", "GritLM (multi-modality in single decoder-only)", "RouterRetriever (learned per-query routing)", "BGE M3 (self-KD between heads)", "CIKM 2025 (bidirectional self-distillation)"],
      "why_promising": "Each component is proven individually: LACONIC proves sparse heads work in decoder-only LLMs, GritLM proves multi-output decoder-only models don't degrade, RouterRetriever proves learned routing adds +2.1 NDCG@10, and CIKM 2025 proves bidirectional self-KD improves both heads. The combination addresses all three known gaps simultaneously while the staged curriculum (validated by LACONIC, LLM2Vec, NV-Embed) manages the joint optimization difficulty documented by CSplade."
    },
    {
      "idea_sketch": "Anchor enrichment via bidirectional reconstruction (EMNLP 2025) as a pre-training stage for BOTH sparse and dense heads before contrastive fine-tuning",
      "from_papers": ["Bidirectional Reconstruction (EMNLP 2025)", "LACONIC (sparse head curriculum)", "NV-Embed (multi-stage training)"],
      "why_promising": "The reconstruction paper shows EOS representations are semantically impoverished and reconstruction enriches them, but only tests dense embeddings. The paper explicitly asks: 'could this anchor enrichment technique improve sparse representations (e.g., SPLADE heads) in addition to dense embeddings?' If the EOS anchor feeds into both heads, enriching it could provide a shared foundation that makes subsequent joint training more stable."
    },
    {
      "idea_sketch": "NV-Embed's latent attention pooling for the dense head combined with SPLADE vocabulary projection for the sparse head, sharing a decoder-only backbone with bidirectional attention",
      "from_papers": ["NV-Embed (latent attention pooling)", "Mistral-SPLADE (decoder-only sparse)", "LLM2Vec (bidirectional attention enabling)"],
      "why_promising": "Latent attention pooling (+0.6 MTEB points in NV-Embed) produces richer dense representations than EOS pooling, while SPLADE naturally uses per-token logits rather than a single pooled vector — the two heads would use different information extraction strategies from the same hidden states, maximizing complementarity. The architectural asymmetry (pooled dense vs. per-token sparse) also naturally differentiates gradient signals, potentially reducing the training instability CSplade documents."
    },
    {
      "idea_sketch": "Replace vocabulary-projection sparse head with SPLARE's SAE-based sparse head in a unified sparse+dense model, enabling language-agnostic sparse retrieval within the unified architecture",
      "from_papers": ["SPLARE (SAE sparse representations)", "BGE M3 (unified dense+sparse)", "LACONIC (decoder-only sparse baseline)"],
      "why_promising": "SPLARE's SAE features are language-agnostic (addressing SPLADE's tokenizer bias) and extract from intermediate layers (not requiring full forward pass through to logits), which could reduce compute when combined with a dense head that needs the final layer. However, SPLARE's frozen SAE constraint conflicts with bidirectional self-distillation — this tension itself is a research question worth investigating."
    },
    {
      "idea_sketch": "MoR's unsupervised routing signals (Moran coefficient, corpus familiarity) as auxiliary training targets for a learned router head, providing supervision without relevance labels",
      "from_papers": ["MoR (unsupervised routing signals)", "RouterRetriever (learned routing)", "DAT (per-query alpha computation)"],
      "why_promising": "The router head's key challenge is obtaining training signal — you need to know which queries benefit from sparse vs. dense retrieval. MoR's Moran coefficient and pre-retrieval corpus familiarity scores could serve as cheap pseudo-labels for router pre-training before refinement with retrieval feedback. This avoids the chicken-and-egg problem of needing good retrievers to train the router."
    },
    {
      "idea_sketch": "Apply NeurIPS 2024 scaling law methodology (IsoFLOP curves, contrastive entropy) to characterize the joint sparse+dense training budget surface and identify compute-optimal configurations for the 3-stage curriculum",
      "from_papers": ["NeurIPS 2024 Scaling Laws", "LACONIC (multi-stage sparse training)", "SIGIR 2025 (sparse vs dense at scale)"],
      "why_promising": "The scaling laws paper proves contrastive entropy is a smooth proxy for NDCG that enables power-law fitting, but only studies dense retrieval. Extending this to joint objectives would answer: at 200 H100-hours, should you train 3B with more data or 8B with less? Should Stage 1 get 40% or 60% of compute? These are currently guesswork — principled answers would be a contribution independent of the architecture."
    }
  ],
  "method_building_blocks": [
    {
      "tool": "Bidirectional attention enabling for decoder-only LLMs",
      "what_it_does": "Replaces the causal attention mask with an all-ones matrix, allowing all tokens to attend to all other tokens. Converts a decoder-only LLM into a bidirectional encoder at the cost of losing generation capability. Typically requires a brief adaptation step (MNTP or SimCSE) to recalibrate representations.",
      "used_by": ["LLM2Vec", "NV-Embed", "SPLARE", "LACONIC (implicitly via LLM2Vec recipe)"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Both sparse and dense heads in the unified model — essential for the sparse head (SPLADE needs full-context token representations) and beneficial for the dense head (NV-Embed shows clear gains). The key design decision is whether to enable it for the full backbone (losing generation, per GritLM's concern) or only for specific layers."
    },
    {
      "tool": "FLOPS regularization with scheduling",
      "what_it_does": "Penalizes the L1 norm of sparse representations (sum of activation magnitudes) to control sparsity. Can be applied with linear or quadratic scheduling (λ increases during training) to allow the model to first learn good representations, then compress them. Controls the sparsity-effectiveness tradeoff at training time.",
      "used_by": ["Mistral-SPLADE (quadratic scheduling)", "SPLARE", "LACONIC"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "The sparse head in the unified model — critical for training stability. CSplade documents that without proper regularization scheduling, ReLU sparse heads in causal LMs diverge. The quadratic schedule from Mistral-SPLADE is the current best practice. Could also serve as an implicit curriculum signal: early training focuses on representation quality, late training focuses on efficiency."
    },
    {
      "tool": "Latent attention pooling",
      "what_it_does": "Replaces EOS-token or mean pooling with a cross-attention layer that attends token representations to a set of trainable prototype vectors (512 in NV-Embed), then mean-pools over the attended outputs. Acts as a learned dictionary that extracts task-relevant features before pooling, addressing the EOS token's lack of pre-trained semantic content.",
      "used_by": ["NV-Embed"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "The dense head of the unified model — provides richer pooling than EOS or mean approaches. Could also be used with different prototype sets for different tasks (retrieval vs. classification) or even for the router head (a small set of 'routing prototypes' that extract query-type features). Adds only ~2M parameters for 512 prototypes."
    },
    {
      "tool": "Adaptive margin self-distillation",
      "what_it_does": "Uses cosine similarity between positive and negative document embeddings (from the model itself) as an adaptive margin target in triplet loss: margin = (1+cos(d⁺,d⁻))/2. Hard negatives automatically receive larger margins, achieving implicit hard negative mining without explicit mining infrastructure.",
      "used_by": ["ICTIR 2025 Adaptive Margins"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Cross-head distillation in the unified model: the sparse head's similarity scores could define adaptive margins for the dense head's contrastive loss (and vice versa). This extends the single-head self-distillation to a bidirectional cross-head signal, providing richer supervision than standard KL-divergence distillation used in BGE M3."
    },
    {
      "tool": "Echo embedding technique",
      "what_it_does": "Feeds input text twice (concatenated) through a causal LM, extracting representations only from the second occurrence where tokens have full bidirectional context via causal attention over the first copy. Avoids modifying the attention mask but doubles sequence length.",
      "used_by": ["Mistral-SPLADE"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "An alternative to full bidirectional attention enabling — preserves generation capability (unlike LLM2Vec's mask replacement) at the cost of 2× sequence length. Could be used selectively for the sparse head while the dense head uses standard causal attention with EOS pooling, avoiding the all-or-nothing bidirectional conversion."
    },
    {
      "tool": "Post-hoc Top-K sparsity selection",
      "what_it_does": "Decouples sparsity level from training by selecting the top-K highest-activation dimensions at inference time rather than using a fixed threshold. Allows a single trained model to serve different sparsity-latency tradeoffs without retraining.",
      "used_by": ["SPLARE (k=40 queries, k=400 documents)"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "The sparse head of the unified model — enables deployment flexibility where the same model can serve low-latency (Top-32) and high-quality (Top-256) sparse retrieval. Also simplifies the router design: instead of binary sparse-vs-dense routing, the router could output a continuous sparsity level K in addition to the sparse-dense interpolation weight."
    },
    {
      "tool": "Unsupervised pre-retrieval routing signals",
      "what_it_does": "Computes per-query features predictive of which retrieval modality will perform best, without requiring relevance labels. Includes corpus familiarity (query embedding distance to K-means centroids), Moran spatial autocorrelation of retrieved documents, and post-retrieval document-corpus alignment.",
      "used_by": ["MoR"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Pre-training signal for the router head — these features could serve as pseudo-labels or auxiliary objectives during router training, bootstrapping the router before retrieval feedback is available. Could also be computed at training time to create query-type-aware training batches."
    },
    {
      "tool": "Bidirectional reconstruction for EOS enrichment",
      "what_it_does": "Trains the model to reconstruct documents from query EOS embeddings (Q2D) and queries from document EOS embeddings (D2Q), forcing the EOS representation to encode rich semantic content before contrastive fine-tuning. The reconstruction difficulty provides a curriculum signal (harder reconstruction = more expressive embeddings).",
      "used_by": ["Bidirectional Reconstruction (EMNLP 2025)"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Stage 0 or Stage 1 pre-training in the curriculum — enriches EOS representations that both the dense head (which pools from EOS) and potentially the sparse head (which may use EOS as a global context signal) depend on. Could replace or augment the MNTP step from LLM2Vec."
    }
  ],
  "common_pitfalls": [
    "Training sparse heads with ReLU activations in causal LMs without proper regularization scheduling causes instability and mode collapse (documented by CSplade). Always use FLOPS regularization with quadratic or linear warm-up scheduling, and monitor sparse activation statistics (mean, variance, fraction of zeros) throughout training. The instability is worse in decoder-only models than encoder-only, so encoder-only recipes (standard SPLADE) don't transfer directly.",
    "Evaluating only on MS MARCO in-domain performance while neglecting BEIR zero-shot transfer. ICTIR 2025's self-distillation paper achieved strong MS MARCO results but dropped to 0.341 BEIR nDCG@10 (vs 0.430 for TAS-B), severely weakening its claims. AAAI reviewers in the IR track expect BEIR evaluation as table stakes; omitting it signals potential overfitting.",
    "Using fixed-weight interpolation as the 'hybrid' baseline rather than per-query adaptive weighting. DAT, MoR, and RouterRetriever all demonstrate +2-7% gains from adaptive weighting — if your learned router doesn't substantially outperform these adaptive baselines (not just fixed interpolation), the contribution is incremental. The real baseline fear test is against DAT-style per-query oracle weighting, not α=0.5 interpolation.",
    "Claiming 'combination novelty' without exhaustive ablations proving the interaction effect. NV-Embed nearly got rejected despite ICLR Spotlight because reviewers noted 'limited novelty of individual components.' The save was systematic ablations. If your contribution is 'first to combine dual heads + self-KD + routing in decoder-only,' you need ablation tables showing: (1) dual heads without self-KD, (2) dual heads without routing, (3) single head with routing, etc. Every component must demonstrate non-trivial contribution.",
    "Ignoring inference latency and index size in favor of pure effectiveness metrics. RepLLaMA's 135GB index was flagged as impractical. SPLARE's intermediate-layer extraction was praised for efficiency. Report: FLOPs per query/document encoding, index size per million documents, queries-per-second on standard hardware, and compare against both sparse-only and dense-only single-head baselines. The unified model must justify its overhead.",
    "Over-investing in the 8B model when the 3B model may be compute-optimal. The NeurIPS 2024 scaling laws paper shows 'the optimal model size is often counter-intuitively NOT the largest available,' and RepLLaMA found '13B variant shows marginal gains over 7B.' Within a 200 H100-hour budget, running all three curriculum stages on 8B leaves little room for hyperparameter search. Consider: 3B with thorough HP search may beat 8B with defaults.",
    "Enabling full bidirectional attention without understanding the tradeoffs. LLM2Vec, NV-Embed, and SPLARE all remove the causal mask, but GritLM shows you can keep causal for generation and switch to bidirectional for embedding. If you enable bidirectional globally, you lose the option to use the model for generation (ruling out future RAG extensions). Consider per-head attention strategies or the Echo embedding alternative from Mistral-SPLADE.",
    "Relying on synthetic data from proprietary models (GPT-4) for training data generation. E5-Mistral's reliance on GPT-4 for 500k synthetic pairs raised reproducibility concerns, and synthetic-only training dropped BEIR from 56.9 to 46.9. Use publicly available training data (MS MARCO, sentence-transformer pairs like Mistral-SPLADE) or document the synthetic generation process reproducibly. AAAI reviewers value reproducibility."
  ],
  "field_trajectory": "The field is converging on three macro-directions. First, **decoder-only unification**: the encoder-only era is ending. LLM2Vec, GritLM, LACONIC, RepLLaMA, NV-Embed, and E5-Mistral collectively prove that decoder-only LLMs match or exceed purpose-built encoders for retrieval, and GritLM shows they can simultaneously serve as generators. The next frontier is unifying sparse+dense retrieval within these decoder-only backbones — BGE M3 proved multi-modality works in encoder-only, but no one has done it for decoder-only, and the TMLR survey explicitly identifies this gap. Second, **learned query-adaptive retrieval**: the fixed-weight hybrid era is ending too. DAT, MoR, and RouterRetriever all demonstrate substantial gains from per-query adaptation, and the trend is toward more tightly integrated routing (from separate model ensembles to intra-model routing). The natural endpoint is a single model with a lightweight router head that adaptively weights its own sparse and dense outputs — exactly the proposed architecture. Third, **efficiency-driven design**: the SIGIR 2024 scaling laws paper's insight that inference cost dominates retrieval budgets is reshaping how systems are designed. SPLARE's intermediate-layer extraction, GritLM's embedding reuse, and post-hoc Top-K sparsity selection all reflect a shift from 'maximize NDCG' to 'maximize NDCG per FLOP.' Papers that demonstrate efficiency gains alongside effectiveness gains (like GritLM's >60% RAG speedup) receive outsized attention. A unified sparse+dense model that halves index size and memory vs. maintaining two separate models would be a compelling efficiency story independent of any effectiveness gains.",
  "meta_advice": "If I were advising a PhD student targeting AAAI 2026 with this research direction, I'd say: your core contribution must be crystal clear — you are the first to produce both sparse and dense representations from a single decoder-only LLM with a learned per-query router, and the combination is non-trivially better than its parts. Frame it as filling the gap the TMLR 2025 survey identifies, validated by PromptReps' zero-shot feasibility proof but with trained heads that dramatically outperform prompting. **Do not let the router become the whole story** — the dual-head architecture with bidirectional self-distillation is the deeper contribution; the router is the cherry on top. Structure your experiments in four tiers: (1) dual heads vs. separate single-head models on the same backbone (proves unification doesn't hurt), (2) with vs. without bidirectional self-distillation (proves cross-head knowledge transfer helps both), (3) learned router vs. fixed interpolation vs. oracle per-query weighting (proves the router captures meaningful query-level signals), (4) full system vs. BGE M3, NV-Embed, and separate LACONIC+RepLLaMA ensemble (proves end-to-end competitiveness). Spend 60% of your compute budget on the 3B model to nail the training recipe, then scale to 8B once — the NeurIPS scaling laws paper suggests 3B may actually be compute-optimal at your budget, and reviewers will appreciate the principled decision. Run BEIR, MTEB retrieval subset, AND MS MARCO; report latency, index size, and FLOPs alongside NDCG. Most critically: the bidirectional self-distillation between sparse and dense heads in a decoder-only backbone is completely novel — this is where you should invest the most analytical effort (gradient analysis, representation similarity, training dynamics plots), because it's the component reviewers cannot evaluate by analogy to existing work. If that mechanism produces even a +1 NDCG@10 improvement on BEIR while being theoretically motivated (complementary error patterns, per SIGIR 2025's 10.5% gap finding), you have a strong submission."
}
```
